My attending physician told me about one of his articles on strange research entitled: ‘Money produces brain activations, especially if others receive less’.
That is to say, the feelings generated by the monthly payment of workers depends on what colleagues earn.
The study was carried out using tomographic magnetic resonances at the University of Bonn, and the results are published in the latest issue of Science.
According to Bernd Weber, author of the study and director of the group in charge of neurocognition images at the Life&Brain Institute in Germany: “The strongest activation occurred when the participant answered correctly and his companion failed.”
The researchers observed that when the tworty people were guessing that they received the same paid, the activation was moderate, but if one of them received double, the activation was greater.
I don't know if this conclusion required so much science.
Prosecutor of the Balkans
Carla del Ponte for eight years has been relentlessly pursuing Balkan war criminals from the Public Prosecutor's Office of the Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia in The Hague.
His term ends at the end of the year and Carla will go away with a bitter taste, because the main perpetrators of Srebrenica's murder - in which 8,000 Bosnian Muslims are dead - ex-General Ratko Mladic and ex-leader Serbobosnio Radovan Karadzic, are still at large.
Their names are in this list whose documentary by the Swiss director Marcel Schüpbach takes its title, 'Carla''s list (2006, not screened exclusively in Spain), with the rhythm of a police thriller, which follows the work of the Swiss prosecutor from Belgrade in New York who is chasinging fugitive criminals and pressuring the governments involved to shrink the circle.
Which insurance is right for me?
Automobile insurance, which covers the driver in the face of possible claims, involves a significant expense that will depend on various factors, such as age, sex, seniority of the driver's license, marital status, the type of vehicle to be insured or the use made of it, among others.
Having a car is the main thing.
Yes, escoger el seguro de automóvil adecuado es incluso más difícil que elegir el modelo de vehículo.
And it is that, choosing adequate car insurance, is even more difficult than choosing the vehicle model.
Car insurance is an agreement between an insurance company and the insurer.
This compromise is reflected in an insurance contract, which establishes that the company receives the value of the premium granted and in exchange it becomes responsible for the obligation to pay compensation in the event of a claim.
The premium is the price of insurance, which is established taking into account various factors, such as age, sex, seniority of the driver's licence, marital status, the type of vehicle to be insured or the use made of it, among others.
Insurance companies make their prices according to the statistics, so that the higher the probability of an accident, the higher the rate to be paid.
It is for this reason that people over the age of 25 and women have to pay lower premiums, since statistics show that these people have a lower loss rate due to caution.
In the same way, by using censuses, the companies have concluded that married people tend to have fewer accidents than single drivers.
The use made of the vehicle will affect the frequency, and therefore the probability of having an accident.
By increasing the frequency the probabilities increase.
There are many of the variables that insurers take into account when pricing the premium.
That's why the driver has to carefully study the situation to choose the car insurance that best adapts to his profile.
Here we will expose the most significant variables when choosing one or the other type of insurance, so that it, in addition to adapting to the characteristics of the insurer, offers it a reasonable price.
Young people under the age of 25 have to deal with a variety of inconveniences when entering into a contract with insurance at an affordable price, as can be the low experience in driving and seniority of the driver's license.
Although the ideal way to be well-assured is insurance at any risk, there are other more economical options.
The most predictive drivers can opt for insurance any risk with deductible, so that they get the coverages of insurance at any risk but at a more affordable price.
This form of agreement implies that the client pays part of the amount of the claim.
"The part of the amount of the loss that the customer pays will be based on the value of the premium, which will be higher the amount will be low," says Luis Noguera, the head in Spain of First Europa, online insurance mediator.
Another option is extended third-party insurance, so that the customer provides protection to deal with damage caused to third parties, but for those caused on his own car.
In addition, it would be covered against the theft, fires and loss of the vehicle.
Some advice.
If you’re a 25-year-old minor trying to be careful and avoid claims as much as possible, since it’s going to have an impact on the bonus, which can reduce the price of insurance in the future.
A bonus is a reduction in the company’s basic rate.
On the contrary, a penalty is an increase in the basic rate.
"Benefications and sanctions will depend on the company and its profit.
If the company has had a good year it will be able to allow itself to increase the subsidies, "says Noguera.
Beyond the age of 25 it becomes easier for drivers when making a contract with insurance, since the loss rate is reduced thanks to the accumulated experience.
That’s why it’s best to choose insurance that’s all-risk deductible.
"Normally young people cannot take advantage of the previous experience acquired after an accident," says the director of the European company in Spain and adds that the majority of accidents occur during the second year of driving, once the 'fear of the novice' has passed.
However, if the client is married but does not yet have children it is preferable for him to negotiate insurance at any risk, as long as his economic situation allows it.
Children's marriages are considered by insurers to be people with the lowest loss rate, because surcharges are lower.
In this case the best insurance would be insurance at any risk with deductible (possibly being 300 euros), or extended third-party insurance from the third year of life of the vehicle when the insured has a high level of bonus.
“They’re driving more casually and they have greater purchasing power to afford the luxury of taking on a larger amount of deductions,” Noguera said.
For retirees insurance at any risk with deductible is preferable (by being this one of 150 euros), or extended third-party insurance when the insurer has a high level of subsidy.
It must be said that car insurance is something important, not only because it covers the driver in the face of possible claims, but also because it represents a significant expense, which is why it is essential to properly study the insurance that suits us most according to our personal situation.
Celtel will be looking to win a point in Milan to reach the eighth of the finals
Milan AC and Celtel Glasgow arise next Tuesday in San Siro in search of the point they lack to achieve their respective objectives in the closure of the Champions League, since it is used for the Italians to be first and the Scots to classify themselves.
The Italians total ten points and they have already qualified in the fifth day thanks to a draw in Portugal, while Celtic Glasgow have nine points, but he still needs another point to ensure that Shakhtar Donetsk, who has six, and who receives Benfica who has four, does not pass.
These two Group D matches will be played a week before the sixth day ends, as Milan AC must play the semi-finals of the Club World Cup in Japan on 13 of this month in search of a place for the December 16 final.
Thus, this group will remain closed before the draw at the Giuseppe Meazza stadium seems to be the most anticipated result, although both teams will try to emerge victorious, since the Italians will not want to speculate at home and Celtic Glasgow knows that by winning he would remain first and ensure a better crossing in the eighth finals.
A defeat of Gordon Strachan's team could be enough to rank if Shakhtar Donetsk doesn't win, but she won't just be able to computer on that since with nine points the game goes back to the Ukrainians, who lost last week in Glasgow (2-1), but won (2-0) in the other direct duel.
Thus, the Milanese will try to poison the evening to the Scots, who are hopeful to finish for the eighth finals for the second time in the Champions League, after having been beaten during the last edition by a goal of Kaka during overtime (1-0).
Waiting for the 'rossoneri' victory the triumph would only return to the Shakhtar, in the event that Celtic lost, to finish for the eighth finals to be able to continue the UEFA Champions League.
And it is that Benfica, although it is last and without the opportunity to continue the European competition, could reach third place with a victory.
20,000 people attend Vancouver at First Concert of Spice Girls Tour
The Spice Girls introduced in Vancouver (Canada), ahead of about 20,000 spectators, their first tour since the band disbanded in 2000.
' Posh Spice' (Victoria Beckham), 'Scary Spice' (Melanie Brown), 'Baby Spice' (Emma Bunton), 'Sporty Spice' (Melanie Chisholm) and 'Ginger Spice' (Geri Halliwell) said shortly before starting their concert at the GM Place stadium in Vancouver that the group's meeting is "brilliant".
Around 20,000 people have exhausted the tickets to attend the concert that was the subject of a video in which is told the story of the band that was all the rage at the end of 1990.
The meeting and world tour coincide with the launch of a new album that resumes their main hits as well as two new songs, 'Headlines' and 'Voodoo', that EMI publishes in Spain.
The five members of the Spice Girls took to the stage by showing the bracelets that 'Posh Spice's husband had given them, British footballer David Beckham.
The athlete was in the public filling the GM Place, even if Victoria forbade him a few days earlier.
Ahead of the show, Victoria Beckham remarked that the British footballer "didn't see our costumes so I'm nervous because I don't know what he's going to think of the show".
'Sporty Spice ' who left the group in 1998 "because of the differences between us" and which provoked the dissolution of the group two years later, also expressed its nervousness during an interview with the press, two hours before the beginning of the show.
“I was afraid.
“I was nervous, but I saw that people’s feelings have changed, the feeling for the Spice Girls has changed.
And I thought why not, I'm happy to have made a decision," Chisholm said of the group meeting she dissolved nine years ago.
The singer also noticed that over time, "things have worked out and that we appreciate each other a lot."
But a lot has changed since the Spice Girls last exhibited in 1998.
To begin with, according to CTV television, the group arrived in Vancouver on a special Virgin Airlines plane.
Upon their arrival at the airport, a babysitter army was waiting for them.
She has five seven sons.
Following the concert in Vancouver, the band will exhibit in California (USA), San José (December 4) and Los Angeles (December 5 and 7).
In Madrid they will be exhibited on 23 December.
The new gas case between Russia and Ukraine raises the alarm.
A dispute over the price of gas ferments between Ukraine and Russia, thus increasing the risks of repercussions on fuel exports to Europe.
Most Russian gas exports to the European Union (U.E) are conveyed by pipelines, and each pipe is scrupulously monitored.
Kiev has announced that if Russia increases the cost of fuel, it will increase transit taxes to Russia.
Last year, an altercation between the two, had caused a decrease in supplies to EU countries.
The last dispute between Kiev and Moscow comes after the Ukrainian government and Gazprom, Russia’s property monopoly company supplying the gas, tried in vain, to find an agreement on the cost of Russian gas supplied to Ukraine for the year 2008.
Gazprom said it would rise as expected, its prices to Ukraine as it agreed to pay for 2008, up to 50% more for the supply of gas from neighboring Turkmenistan.
Analysts said this would include for Ukraine, a 40% increase in import costs from Gazprom.
"It is essential for us to use all our arguments during the conversations, recallinging that we are a country through which most of the gas transits," Ukrainian Economy Minister Anatoly Kinkh told Reuters news agency.
“Fluctuations in gas costs would probably be linked to our discussions about transit taxes (to Western Europe) via Ukraine”
The last major altercation between Moscow and Kiev caused in January 2006 the interruption of Gazprom's supplies to Ukraine.
This leaded to the fall in exports to Western Europe, thereby increasing prices.
Russian gas accounts for a quarter of EU supplies.
Russia has the largest stocks of gas, even though most of them are still underdeveloped.
As a result, Gazprom complements its own supplies with gas from Turkmenistan and other nations of the former Soviet Union in Central Asia.
The Ministry of Health "must monitor its foreign employees".
The Scottish Ministry of Health needs to improve controls on its extra-community employees to check whether they have previous criminals, the control committee said.
Revisiver General's warning, Robert Black, came after a foreign doctor working in Scotland was involved in the June attack at Glasgow airport.
In addition, Mr Black expressed concern that the Scottish Department of Health has no specific data on the number of overseas people it employs.
Nevertheless, his report highlighted other decisive checks, correctly carried out on a sample of employees of five health authorities.
The reviser-general carried out checks on extra-community personnel following the Glasgow airport attack.
Bilal Addullah, 28, a doctor of Iraqi descent at the Royal Alexandra Hospital in Paisley, was arrested at the airport after rushing with his jeep into the doors of the main terminal.
He was one of the three doctors who were filmed at the Old Bailey in October because he was suspected of being involved in various attempts to explode cars in the UK.
The Ministry of Health will have to carry out pre-employment checks on overseas personnel, in order to be sure of the validity of their qualifications, experiences, and work permits for the United Kingdom.
Although the Scottish Ministry of Health is using the Scottish Disclosure to carry out checks on previous criminals, the agency has no right to obtain such information outside of Britain.
Among the many warnings, the Auditor General's report now states that committees are required to confront the Office of Supervisory of Previous Criminals, thus allowing employers to undertake controls over various countries.
"While we have discovered that some information about staff has been audited, this is not done every time," the report said.
Mr Black said that the Scottish Department of Health carries out most of the pre-employment checks it would do itself, but he also added: “Our report highlights aspects where the system can be improved to ensure that adequate information about some overseas staff is available to health services.
Peter Terry, president of BMA Scotland, said it is "extremely unlikely" that pre-employment checks could identify employees who could commit future terrorist acts.
“The report suggests that the pre-employment checks currently carried out, work well in the Scottish health system and that it is not certain that these warnings can bring any added value.”
Scottish health authorities have reported employing 1161 overseas people, most of whom are doctors and nurses.
However, the reviser General stressed that these figures certainly need to be revised upwards, in part because of the problems relating to the identification of non-determined contract staff, who may or may not choose to remain in our country.
The investigation has shown that the current control procedures of health authorities in Ayrshire and Arran, Grampian, Greater Glasgow and Clyde, Lanarkshire, and Lothian, are quite adequate.
These checks are carried out on passports, work permits and qualifications.
The Scottish Government is currently revising its policy and pre-employment control system for staff members outside the ocean of the health service.
Health Secretary Nicola Sturgeon said: “I was excited to carry out this work in the wake of the terrorist attack at Glasgow airport.”
It added that the Scottish Government allied to the Health Committees would carefully consider the warnings set out in the report to be published next month and would undertake to ensure a better implementation of recently updated pre-employment controls that are valid for all employees, including overseas persons.
Joy Division dominates the Oscars.
Control, the bio-peak on the last singer of Joy Division, Ian Curts, won 5 prizes, including the best film, at the Oscars of the British Independent Film.
The trophies were also awarded to star Sam Riley, who worked in a store until he was engaged in the role of Curtis in this film whose director is Anton Corbijn and co-star Toby Kebbell.
Judi Dench was named Best Actress for Her Role in the thriller "Notes on A Scandal"
Daniel Craig, the star Bond has won the Variety Award for allowing the British film to be under international reflectors.
The prizes were distributed during the ceremony at London's Round house.
Control chronicles Curtis' rise to success with Joy Division, until his suicide in 1980, aged 23.
The black-and-white film, whose band was directed by The Killers, David Bowie and New Order, was shot with a budget of just 3 million Sterling.
But the Dutch photographer, best known for his iconic cover of U2's Joshua Tree album, won two awards, the first as the best director and the second as the best beginning director.
Rock has also been rewarded with Best Documentary awarded to Julien Temple’s film on Clash singer Joe Strummer.
The Best Actor Award was presented to Viggo Mortensen for his portrayal of the driver of a Russian mafia boss, in the Londrès-based thriller directed by David Cronenberg, Easter Promises.
Ray Winstone renowned for his coriacidal portrayals, won the Richard Harris Prize for his remarkable contribution to the British film.
The fiftieth-year director, having shot films like Scum, Nil By Mouth and Sexy Beast, is currently filming the sequel of Indiana Jones, The Crystal Skull Kingdom.
Winstone said he was proud to receive this award named in honor of star Harris, protagonist in Camelot and Harry Potter, who died in 2002.
Richard Harris and Albert Finney allowed people like me to have access to cinema,” he said.
“I’ve been lucky enough to meet Richard Harris twice.”
"I was admiring in front of this current great actor, I was extremely honored."
The Oscars of the independent film, better known as "the Bifas" are awarded by a jury composed of directors, actors and festival organizers.
Bifa’s fans and bosses include Mike Figgis, Ken Loach, Ewan McGregor, Samantha Morton, Bill Nighy, Meera Syal and Michael Winterbottom
Saudi blocks 208 terrifying plots
Saudiradi revealed yesterday that it had arrested 208 militants involved in organizing cells planning an impending attack on an oil site, as well as attacks on ecclesiasticals and armed forces.
The state television of the world's largest oil exporter, said one of the missile smuggler cells.
Al Qaeda sympathizers have staged a campaign against the allied monarchony in the United States since 2003, following the invasion of Iraq.
A cell composed of eight militants, headed by a foreign resident was planning an attack on an oil site in the Eastern Province, the TV newspaper said.
Saudi Arabia quickly formed a group of 35,000 soldiers to protect the sites, following a failed attack by Al Qaeda in 2006, against the largest oil site in Abqaiq.
The newspaper citing a statement by the interior minister, said 18 of those arrested belonged to a cell led by a “missile-launching expert” who infiltrated the country.
Activists are said to be planning the counterband of eight missiles in the kingdom.
22 of the detainees appear to have plotted the killing of ecclesiasticals and armed forces.
The government has asked the ecclesiastics to do more against the Saudis led in Iraq, to join Al Qaeda militants fighting against the Allied Forces and against the allied Muslim Shiite government in the United States, considered heretical according to the hard line of the Saudi Sunny.
Activists from Al Qaeda believe that in Saudi Arabia, many ecclesiasticals have collaborated with the authorities to support the policy of the royal family that dominates the government.
Activists, encouraged by appeals against the pro-Western Saudi government, by Saudi-bosted Al Qaeda leader Osama Bin Laden targeted foreign residential areas, government buildings and energy supply sites.
Significant efforts have been made by the armed forces over the past five months," the spokesman for the Minister of the Interior told state television.
Among the inmates are 16 members of the Medina organized into a “media cell” to promote “takfiri thought,” the ideology of Sunni Muslim radicals that supports the use of violence against unfaithful and apostate Muslims.
Of the detainees, 32 are accused of subsidizing the activists.
The climate of Venus could reveal the future of the Earth.
Scientists said yesterday that Venus has certainly been a pleasant planet.
Data from the European spacecraft in orbit have shown that the planet is certainly twice as small as Earth, contrary to what was previously thought.
Venus is the closest planet to Earth.
These two worlds are practically the same size, with similar orbits, although Venus is closer to the Sun.
Scientists now believe that billions of years ago, Venus may have had conditions similar to those of the Earth, and may even have been habitable.
But the greenhouse effect due to carbon dioxide, atmospheric erosion due to solar radiation combined with slower rotation has led Venus to a whole other kind of evolution.
Today the planet is deprived of water, is ravaged by hurricanes and lightning storms and has a temperature surface warm enough to melt lead.
The findings of Venus Express, the probe of the European Space Agency, which studied the planet for a year, offers a sad vision of Earth’s potential future due to catastrophic climate changes.
The professor in the Department of Physics at the University of Oxford, Fred Taylor, one of the leaders of the Venus Express mission, said: “We’re starting to understand why the climate of Venus is so different from that of Earth, although the planets themselves are virtually similar.
“Our new data allows us to establish a scenario according to which billions of years ago, Venus was originally identical to the Earth, perhaps even with a habitable surface.”
Enriched uranium sequestered by the police.
A kilogram of radioactive material, apparently of uranium, was sequestered by the Czechoslovak and Hungarian police yesterday.
One policeman said that three people have been arrested and that the equipment is currently being analysed.
He did not confirm that it is indeed uranium, but revealed that the goods would have a value of $ 1 million (£480,000).
Still open to everything
Mitt Romney said he didn’t live up to the pitch as a presidential candidate, to answer questions from a snowman.
Despite this, Republican presidential candidates have finally agreed to participate in You Tube’s debate, during which many ordinary voters who have recorded their questions submit them to candidates.
On Wednesday evening, November 28, none of the questions asked were as ridiculous as that of another debate of You Tube, when a snowman asked the Democrats their position in relation to global warming.
But this time, the questions and answers have greatly reflected the conditions of the Republican Party, candidate in the next election.
We don’t know on what criteries, the CNN producers, who sponsored the debate, chose the clips of You Tube to show the aspiring candidates.
But the first half hour of the debate, the longest approaching a single theme, focused on immigration.
The topic is one of the strong topics for Republicans.
CNN asked some voters to phone when they heard funny things: The board picked up increases in endorsement when the candidates were speaking rudely.
The hardest and longest exchange of the evening was between Rudy Giuliani, leader of the national front, and Mr. Romney leading the important early elections in Iowa and New Hampshire.
Giuliani tried to highlight the fact that he was accommodating about illegal immigration during his tenure as mayor of New York.
He attacked Mr. Romney, accusing the former governor of Massachusetts, of employing illegal immigrants at home.
Both fighters became particularly ferocious when asked if they would be willing to promote “amnesia” (offeringing clandestines regular status and the opportunity to become a citizen).
Minor candidates like Duncan Hunter, a California congressman, and Tom Tancredo, a congressman from Colorado, managed to deepen this topic.
None of them has the opportunity to be elected, but both remain in the race, perhaps thanks to the fact that they have begun to fight immigration before and more incisively than the others.
In general, candidates, at least with regard to immigration, have the same opinions: more rigid borders, and zero tolerance for illegal immigrants.
This topic, allied with the concerns raised in particular by the case of toxic toys made in China, or many other fears about the external world, favor the Republicans to forget, or to minimize the divisions between libertarians and Catholic conservatives.
But the debate became particularly embarrassing when other issues were raised.
Giuliani asked why he had previously backended the gun control campaign.
John McCain and Romney debated whether putting water in the nose should be considered torture.
One of the speakers asked whether the candidates believed in the contents of the Bible in its entirety.
This led Mr. Giuliani dared to declare that the Bible is his favorite book, even if it is not certain to believe the story of Jonah and the Whale.
Another asked whether Jesus would have been in favor of the death penalty.
Mike Huckabee was not expected to be in pole position in Iowa in a recent election, thanks to his long-time Christian conservatism, that the death penalty is painful although necessary.
When insisted on whether Jesus would have been in favor, he laughed and said that Jesus is too classy to run in elections.
The human race remains unusually open to everything.
Huckabee’s comeback in Iowa coincides with the prodigious searches for grants conducted by libertarian Ron Paul, and the whistles during Wednesday’s debate.
These two are emerging from nothingness, thanks to the fact that the two protégés of the party fail to agree to decide who is the best-known and more traditionalist candidate.
In 1980 and 2000, a candidate unified the party early and convincingly thanks to a message of success on American greatness thus bringing the leaders closer together.
This time, the party is divided and the result is far from clear.
The pains of Labors
Gordon Brown discovered a new hole to sink into; even if he resigned, the situation wouldn't change.
In view of the trend of British scandals, it is not particularly juicy.
No decorations sold, no Parisian hotel bills of politicians, no extra-marital foot finger sucked.
But now a revelation attests that a wealthy businessman would have masked for four years, under false names, his contributions to the current Labour Party, equivalent to more than £6,50,000 ($1.3m), that the party would not have declared: A new battle from which Gordon Brown did well.
Two months ago, the Prime Minister backtracked in his draft election, foolishly insulting British intelligence proclaiming that opinion polls did not influence his decision, and on the contrary he hoped to offer a compact and effective government.
On the contrary, such as Vince Cable, the brilliant Liberal Democratic leader, stated that the terrifying Mr. Brown turned from Stalin to Mr. Bean.
The acquisition of Northern Rock, a troubled bank on the edge of crack, has demonstrated a flaw of guidance and poor judgment in case of an emergency.
Inadequate proposals in favor of changing the tax code have provoked a coalition of industrial groups against the government.
The loss of 25 million personal data from the tax authorities has revealed an incredible administrative blunder.
A trio of retired generals launched a revolt among the Lords, protesting against reductions in military spending: they proclaimed that being a defence minister is for them, a part-time job.
At present, the suspicion of shady donations by David Abrahams; Peter Watt, the General Secretary of the Labours (and his ex-chief of vigilance) have meant that they have had to resign.
None of this means that Mr. Brown is inevitably heading towards the exit.
However, governments can get to a point where it would be impossible to govern.
People don't like politicians, and don't trust themselves, but generally leave their doubts behind when a new government comes to power.
It seems obvious that a Prime Minister can not improve things, or even govern for a long time, when he risks the suspension: the public administration no longer works; the cabinets of the ministers take stock; the press is looking for the wrong stories; and the government becomes defensive and without any more objective.
In a sense, Mr. Brown's position is particularly difficult, because unlike other political leaders, he can't brag about being a beginner.
Although he would like to repudiate Tony Blair, he was still his senior lieutenant.
Problems relating to Northern Rock and tax data, for example, are related to Mr. Brown's decisions when he was Chancellor of the British Treasury.
And even if he is not directly involved in Blair’s misfortunes, such as Blair’s lengthy investigation into the party’s funds “cash for members of the House of Lords,” voters might think otherwise.
But Mr. Brown's difficult situation isn't as tragic as she seems.
Blunders aren't so monstrous like members of the Torian Party make him believe.
As in Mr. Bean, there's no casualties or scams.
And the remedy, though painful, could be to concretize Mr. Brown's Stalinist tendencies forever, in favor of a reliable and slightly brutal government.
The new scandal over political donations is a good example.
Mr. Abrahams, a very particular figure, who compares his donations to charity, said he did not want any public recognition of his benevolence.
He may have his opinion, but it's not up to him to decide.
Laws against the anonymity of the big subsidiers of politicians are clear, and they exist for good reason: The public has the right to know who contributes to the success of those who will govern the country.
Mr. Watt, who stated that he was aware of Mr. Abrahams' programs, without knowing that this was illegal, will therefore have to be questioned, along with Jon Mendlesohn, the head of grant collection who had spoken about questionable donations in October.
But so far Mr. Brown is not involved: Nothing suggests that he was aware of these steps, even if most of the payments made were made following his arrival at the government in June.
So far the Prime Minister has done half.
He quickly condemned his party's legislative loophole, but he did not draw attention to the fact that decision-making power is based on something he is familiar with, resorting to an investigative committee on the subject consisting of a retired bishop and a judge.
In this case, the lessons to learn are not complicated.
And if the law has been violated, it is the responsibility of the police.
In addition, there are many other things that Mr. Brown needs to do, such as finding a permanent chairman for the Independent Committee of the Standards of Public Life, with no leader for six months, and preparing a good reform on the financial levels of political parties.
This would force the Labour Party, in a moment of weakness, to collaborate with the Tories, which could lead it to abandon its particular financial relationship with the confederations; in short, a bitter pill for a supporter such as Mr. Brown.
However, he would have to deal with certain partisan facts.
The Conservative Party now has a good advantage for the election; it has two years to win back voters, and the only way to achieve victory is to demonstrate irreproachable integrity and strong skills.
Card scams.
Is the mortgage crisis contaminating the U.S. credit card market?
When there is blood in the water, it is normal to see fanned fins, swirl around.
Now that the U.S. real estate market is going through a critical phase, predators are targeting the U.S. credit card market.
Goldman Sachs analysts believe that credit card losses could reach $99 billion if contagion extends from mortgages to other forms of consumer credit.
There are clear signs of tension.
There are increases in fees and insolvency rates, which measures non-recoverable balance sheet shares, more than 30 days later.
HSBC announced last month that it took $1.4 billion in its credits to the consumer, in part because of the weak of cardholders.
It's too early to panic.
Costs and insolvencies are still moderated.
According to Moody, a tax agency, the 3.89% insolvency rate in the third quarter is almost one point lower than its historical average.
Deterioration of rates is partly explainable by technical factors.
A change in laws in 2005 in the U.S. regarding personal bankruptcy caused a sharp drop in bankruptcy archives, which in turn caused heavy losses to credit cards; the number of archives (and therefore insolvency rates) could increase even if borrowing conditions had to worsen.
In addition, the industry announces fixed payment rates, which highlight the amount of monthly indebtedness that consumers have to settle.
and confidence in credit card capital security is quite strong despite the paralysis of other aspects of structured finance.
Dennis Moroney of TowerGroup, a research company, predicted that the emission volume for 2007 will be 25% higher than last year.
The direct links of infection between the mortgage crisis and the credit card market certainly exist: Consumers may prefer to increase their debt now that fair mortgages have disappeared.
But card issuers pay more attention to the flow of cashes rather than the value of wealth, and therefore the fall in real estate prices has not necessarily led to a change in the credibility of borrowers.
They might even bring benefits to issuers of credit securities.
Consumer premiums for the payment of mortgages fall if properties cost less than the value of the loan; as a result, credit card indebtedness would become a priority to repay.
Card issuers are able to respond much faster than mortgages and with more flexibility to such crisis conditions, as they can change interest rates or impair credit limits.
This should, in theory, reduce the risk of a rapid resumption of actions.
“We’re not going to wake up one morning and re-evaluate all loans,” said Gary Perlin, director of Capital One.
If a financial disaster in the credit card market is unlikely, there is a high risk of a significant decline.
If lower real estate prices and a contraction in the credit market are driving America into recession, the industry will certainly have to face a gruesome future.
Pay attention to the dorsal fins.
British bankers plead culprited for Enron.
Britain’s three bankers who had to testify their roles compared to a fraud committed by Enron’s previous chief financial officer, Andrew Fastow, changed their statement, pleading guilty on Wednesday.
David Bermingham, Giles Darby and Gary Mulgrew had initially declared themselves innocent of the seven accusations of alleged collusory fraud in 2000, committed by Fastow in a secret financial scam in order to enrich themselves in spite of their employees.
They are scheduled to appear in court in January.
However, during a hearing presided over by U.S. Judge Ewing Werlein Jr., they all found themselves guilty of committing fraud.
The three former executives of Greenwich NatWest, a subsidiary of the PLC Group of the Royal Bank of Scotland, have become famous all over Britain because of two-year extradition proceedings.
In the United States, their case remains an outstanding issue for this investigation launched following the fall of Enron in 2001.
The charges inflicted on them in 2002 made Fastow clear that he was the subject of the government's investigation into Enron's debacle.
Charges against the bankers, dubbed the “Natwest Three” claim to have come to Houston in 2000, to stage a fraudulent scenario with Fastow and his ex-right-hand, Michael Kopper.
NatWest Greenwich has invested in a subsidiary of a Fastow-controlled Enron partner, which was the director of many fraudulent scenarios, which helped him rig bankruptcy proceedings.
At the beginning of 2002, the bank had considered its interest in the subsidiary as zero, but in fact the three UKs are already aware of its value.
A Kopper-controlled company bought interest from the subsidiary’s bank for £1 million.
Bankers paid $250,000 to Kopper as an interest in this company.
According to the accuse, Fastow falsely told Enron that the energy company had to pay $20 million to Greenwich NatWest for its shares in the subsidiary.
But in truth these $20 million went into the pockets of British bankers, Fastow and others.
According to the charges, the bankers got $7.3 while Fastow, Kopper and the others earned $12.3 million.
In January 2004, Fastow pleaded guilty to his active role in two conspiracies in Enron's debacle.
The British trio was arrested three months later.
Fastow endures a six-year incarceration in a federal prison in Louisiana.
Kopper is incarcerated for 3 years and one month in a prison in Texarkana.
The three were discharged following the payment of a $1million deposit each, forcing them to live in the United States during the trial.
Enron, formerly the seventh-largest national company, bankrupt in December 2001 after years of accounting rigging, could no longer hide its billions of debt or even offer its bouncy speculation as profitable.
The collapse caused thousands of layoffs, more than $60 billion of loss in market value and more than $2 billion in losses for pension plans.
Enron founder Kenneth Lay and former President General Jeffrey Skilling were convicted last year for their involvement in the societal collapse.
Skilling was sentenced to more than 24 years in prison.
Charges against Lay for conspiracy, fraud and other offenses were in vain because of her death due to a heart attack last year.
No money, not outside Moscow.
The cost of a G550 Gulfstream aircraft, equipped with a Rolls-Royce engine and with sufficient fuel autonomy to fly non-stop from Moscow to Los Angeles, departs from $54 million.
But this is only a small change for some Russians.
"It can carry up to 18 passengers," said Dan Firer, a Gulfstream representative.
“Even though no one really needs so many seats because it’s more of a family-used aircraft or for the president of a company.”
Mr. Firer was present at the Gulfstream stand last Friday at the Salon des Millionaires, an orgy of encouragement for consumption, for four days, held in a huge exhibition center around the capital.
In addition to Gulfstream, more than 200 other exhibitors were present with their stalls, hoping to arouse the interest of the rich Russians for their various products, ranging from mahogany yachts to cars filled with diamonds such as those of 50 Cent.
Mr. Firer was scrutinizing a brilliant orange Porsche, exposed next to his booth.
"it's a nice car, but nothing comparable to our product," he said.
“After the United States, Russia is the second-largest private aircraft market.”
“Thanks to an oil boom, the Russian capital seems to be rolling in black gold, such as Dallas.”
“We experienced the same thing in the 1890’s, when some Americans became so rich and landed in Europe where they were considered materialistic,” said Marshall Goldman, a senior scholar in the Davis Department of Russian and Eurasian Studies at Harvard University.
Like the Saudis in the 70’s, the Japanese in the 90’s, the Russians emerge as the world’s biggest spenders.
The biggest luxury brands are looking for their place in the Klondike.
This week, in another place in Moscow, a conference on the luxury industry allowed Donatella Versace, Tom Ford and Bernard Arnault, president of LVMH, to exchange their ideas on the strategy to follow for the sale of luxury goods to markets like Russia.
(The conference was hosted by the International Herald Tribune, owned by The New York Times.)
Russia has 53 billionaires worth $282 billion, according to Forbes listing the wealthiest people.
In addition, two-thirds of the wealthy are made up of 103,000 Russian millionaires worth overall $670 billion, according to a study on the evolution of personal wealth in the former Soviet Union, carried out by economists at the Center for Economic and Financial Research.
(The World Bank stated that in 2005, 15.8% of Russians lived below the poverty line.)
“There are hundreds of millionaires in Moscow, that’s why we’re all here,” said Nicole Marais, a representative of London’s Davis Morris.
She participated in the show for the first time with the Russian distributor, Le Groupe Bijoutier de Soho.
“Women are all well-prepared and men are worth a fortune.”
Walking in the living room on Sunday night, the two real estate agents put on their 31, Evgenia Yumatova, 21, and Svetlana Turovtsova, 26 claim to be like their clients, millionaires.
“I was looking at the cars and trying to choose between the Infiniti, the Mercedes and the Porsche,” said Ms. Yumatova, who grabbed her haute couture dress like a red carpet veteran like Joan Rivers.
The show seems above all to aim at the new rich, the kind of buyers of Russian brands willing to queue in the corks to come to a huge warehouse where Cadillac Escalades, platinum dyes, Mövenpick ice creams, personalized pool tables, apartments in Dubai and Gund plush bears.
The public could buy tickets for 30 euros ($44.57).
“The Russians are interested in defining the most expensive and best brands,” said Mr. Gijrath, sitting proudly at the Royal Dutch porcelain factory factory stand Delf, in front of a handmade handmade Raimbrant wall board.
“It’s certain that Russians are more interested in bling bling”
Indeed, sellers have stated here that Russians with old capital (10 to 15 years are too according to Russian standards) have already switched from brilliant logos to a kind of limited-edition brand that could not be sold at the show.
Despite this, thousands of people, either millionaires, emissaries of millionaires or tourists, survey the exhibition every day.
And some even buy.
On the first night of the exhibition last Thursday, Roman Ershow, the CEO of King Koil beds, said he sold two sets of silk mattresses, cashmere, and cotton inlaid with golden yarns.
During the first night, Mikhail Lyubiumov, the sales manager of A1 motorcycle, a company that equips cars with state-of-the-art alarm systems, also said it sold to a Russian millionaire, the brilliant orange Porsche, for $170,000.
By Sunday afternoon, Andrei Karapetian, the president of L.A. Connection, a company that personalizes luxury cars, said he would sell three cars similar to bracelets for Rolls-Royce Phantoms at $55,000 each.
He showed a reporter, the wheel of a car designed for the owner of a Russian casino, who wanted it to be inlaid with Swarovski crystals to look like a roulette wheel.
The set of four costs $22,000.
“The Russian millionaire who wants wheels made of ostrich leather, or a picture of his wife made of cubic zirconium, will not necessarily drive with such tires,” Karapetian said.
He’s definitely going to keep it in his garage that already contains 30 or 40 vehicles.”
Even though many of its Russian customers are wealthy, Karapetian said the phrase "Russian millionaire" is not on the agenda, given the rise in property prices here.
“If you had bought an apartment in Moscow three or four years ago, you’d already be a millionaire, at least on paper, if not in cash,” he said.
“There’s more than you might think.”
Those who are not millionaires can at least feel this emotion when they go to the stand of Alliance Continental, a Russian heritage management company.
The company erected four stands representing the British telephone booths, filled with mass of counterfeit dollars.
Azad Karryev, the company’s chief marketing officer, said his team printed 500,000 counterfeit bills for the Millionaire show.
Passers-by arrested to launch masses of banknotes, ask their friends to photograph them on their mobiles, in the middle of this false wealth or pocket a few millions.
“You see, here in Moscow, everyone can feel a millionaire,” Karyev said.
Unapproved surgeon suspected of homicide in New Jersey
It was the eve of Thanksgiving when Ronald Livingston was looking through the bay window of Just a Little Healthier restaurant and was observing an ambulance that had just stopped at the gates of White Cape Cod on West Scott Avenue.
Then came the police patrol.
And then another.
“Then I saw the medical vehicle stop,” Livingston, a restaurant manager, said Wednesday.
That's when I realized that someone had passed away.
Authorities would then learn that the deceased was a 41-year-old woman, Olga Arroyo, an assistant to the Newark Police Department director.
Now investigators are trying to determine whether the cause of his death is due to a gesture performed by an unlicensed cosmetic surgeon, which the police have not yet identified, residing at 212 West Scott Avenue.
The authorities have not yet determined the causes of the death of Ms. Arroyo, but they are waiting confident, the results of the toxicological tests which, unfortunately will take time, but which should make it possible to solve this mysterious death that has saddened the high ranks of the Newark police.
Ms. Arroyo has been Assistant to Police Director Garry F. McCarthy since September 2006, and her voice was well known in various departments.
She was loyal, hard-working and tenacious,” McCarthy said about Ms. Arroyo in a telephone interview on Wednesday.”
“She was like a pit bull.”
“She’d done everything to protect me.”
McCarthy said Ms. Arroyo was married to Jimmy Arroyo, a former Newark police officer who worked for the Alcoholic Beverage Control Board, and that she leaves two sons aged 14 and 11, as well as a daughter-in-law.
Eileen Walsj, spokesman for Union County prosecutor's office, Theodore J. Romankow, said detectives were trying to determine whether Ms. Arroyo died because of unauthorized surgery.
“It’s a possibility to consider.”
“The investigation continues.”
Authorities said they arrived home around 3pm.
and to have found Miss Arroyo on the sofa.
She was declared deceased.
Just more than a week has passed, and the authorities have not said much about the investigation or even about the owner of the house identified as Flor Margarita Castro.
Miss Walsh couldn't confirm whether Ms. Castro was interrogated by the authorities.
Investigators are trying to figure out whether part of the house was being used as a medical practice.
On Wednesday, no one opened the door of Melle Castro’s house where a faded rosier rests in the narrow cemetery in front, and a Dalmatian barked in the back.
A blue can called "Ethyl Vanillin" used as a substitute for vanilla in the kitchen and for perfumes, lay in a shed, next to a door that leads to the basement.
Two all-terrain bicycles hung on the sides.
McCarthy said that since arriving in Newark 25 years ago at the New York Police Department, Ms. Arroyo has been extremely valuable, helping her navigate the new city and abandon her framed political culture.
“She said, ‘You have to pay attention to this,” he said.
“She was there when I arrived, and again when I left.
“It was embarrassing.”
Everyone hopes that the Federal Reserve will lower rates related to the effervescence of the world stock market.
Shares exploded on both sides of the Atlantic yesterday, after the market hoped the Federal Reserve would have tried to calm credit conditions, later reducing interest rates promoted by soothing statements by the U.S. central bank’s vice president.
Stock markets are exploding in Londrès, New York and all over Europe after Donald Kohn, No. 2 after Ben Bernanke, chairman of the Federal Reserve, said those who decide must be “flexible and pragmatic” in their equities in turbulent financial markets.
In an obvious effort to reassure irritable markets given these new credit-related tensions, Kohn said that the recent turmoil “reversed some improvements in the functioning of the market at the end of September and October.
In the event that this harsh turbulence had to persist, it would increase the probabilities of further rigidity of financial conditions for owners and businesses, he said.
These comments appear to investors as a clear signal that the Federal Reserve is ready to inject new capital into U.S. markets, and that further decreases in interest rates could stabilize credit conditions.
These remarks also made it possible to calm Wall Street with even worse news about the U.S. economy, such as the biggest decline in real estate sales for October.
The repercussions of this tumult financial has been accentuated following the statement of Bear Stearns, an investment bank, announcing that it will dismiss 650 people, i.e., 4% of its staff, to reduce the costs to compensate for the heavy losses due to the losing bets of mortgages.
Only 20 positions are planned in Londrès where the bank employs 1500 people.
In New York, the Dow Jones industrial average rose by 331 points, or 2.55% concluding at 13,289.40 while the largest index, S&P 500, took 2.65%.
In London, the FTSE 100 index took 165.5 points or 2.7% concluding at 6306.2 adding more than £39 to the value of British companies.
The German Dax index rose by 2.6% and the French CAC40 gained 2.3%.
Previously, the recurrence of the difficulties of credit in the markets was highlighted following the strong demand for an auction of monthly sorting funds by the European Central Bank, which cut these loans to European commercial banks at a heavy rate of 4.7%, the highest since April 2001.
Pressure on the Bank of England to monitor the EDF and ECB and inject additional funds into London’s financial markets, causing credit pressures, have meant that “Libor” monthly sort borrowing rates for British banks have caused their highest growth since last September to 6.59% following a sharp rise since 12 November.
England faces a call for judgement over criticism of Owais Shah and Ravi Bopara.
We're facing two dilemmas, one as difficult as the other.
Who will be the No. 6 drummer for England in Kandy this weekend, Owais Shah or Ravi Bopara?
And even more important in a context as broad as the first Test against Sri Lanka, who will make the decision?
The second question is important because David Graveney, the president of the coaches who was exceptionally there to help Peter Moores, chief coach, and Michael Vaughan, the captain, make his decision, and because the office of Hugh Morris, England's Chief Executive Officer, is starting to fill out the candidacy forms to become a national coach, at least part-time.
Those who do not manage to win bib No. 6 will be unlucky, but life is often unfair and sport is too.
It's never easy to annihilate what allows a man to get to the top of his profession while another will never override the lower level, sometimes for lack of luck more than for lack of ability.
It's not just cricket that selection is difficult, because in the end, judgment remains subjective.
Is it the right time to make Bopara emerge?
Is that right about Shah?
The same could be proposed in another context, who choose between John Edwards or Banerjee "tubby"; both, new executives having obtained good degrees, professionals, should be promoted: but there is only room for one of them.
Who has the right to determine the right level of a cricker unless at least one chance is given?
The figures are important in decisions, but also opportunities.
Inside a team, a cricker has to capture the captain's eye, at the same time he has to take the door and a drummer must be lucky to touch the one-day cricker.
But capturing the eyes of selectors is much more complicated.
Many did better last season, for example, James Anderson who seemed certain to play the first Test, even though Stephen Harmison turned out in great shape and continued to train to serve.
Anderson took 32 first-class gates out of 33 laps to win a spot.
Chris Tremlett only took 29 to the same average, but both have already experienced their moment of glory at the highest level and still hope to offer.
If it'll be Shah or Bopara playing at Asgiriya Stadium, it's only a matter of opinion.
This choice could annihilate or build a man’s career, but it makes little difference.
Four years ago, in Sri Lanka, Gareth Batty played in every Test and did well in the first, with five gates and 40 useful tricks, but it was Paul Collingwood, who was also struggling to emerge, who managed to become the primordial cricker of the Test, and this, despite the fact that his double one hundred scored against Australia in Australia can still be underestimated.
The probability predicts that Graveney, Vaughan, and Moores, following another discussion after today's session at Asgiriya Stadium, will certainly follow their conservative instincts and promote Shah's greatest experience against the exuberance of the young Bopara.
They both have good arguments.
In Shah’s favor, his brilliant and considerable start at the innings of the Test of 88 in Bombay, 32 cents of first class against six for Bopara, who at the age of 22 is seven years younger, and his excellent ranking at launch.
But Shah made only ten rounds during his two innings in May against the Caribbean during the Test to Lord, his Middlesex-playing city, and that, the immediate rejection by the coaches when Vaughan back in shape, demanded his place for the next game, seems to have afflicted his impulses so obvious in Bombay.
Contrary to him, the Essex player is full of joie de vivre.
Nothing frightens him on a cricket field and he exaggerated when he recently collapsed on a 4x4 during a presentation of Volkswagen, sponsor of England.
He postponed only minor injuries, was shocked, even if the first thing he did was take his Vodafone (sponsor of course) and call Alastair Cook, his partner who was looking at him from afar, to ask him if "he had seen this?"
Bopara is by far the best drummer, his shooting pace allows him to do services that suit him, and that he beats with confidence and professionalism.
Choosing the right man is one thing, choosing the right time is another.
Is this the real art of selection?
I’d be good at giving Bopara a chance, which doesn’t mean Shah shouldn’t apply if, as the tea leaves suggest, he’ll play this weekend.
Buses bring people back to work and fight against al Qaeda.
The first bus full of Iraqi refugees from Syria arrived in Baghdad yesterday, pouring these exiles without money or visa renewals, or still others who hope that the city has regained normalcy.
Some have returned to fight against the militants who distanced them from home.
At Mansour bus station, even before the arrival of Damascus of 800 people transported in convoys of dozens of buses of the official government, the terminal was filled with refugees from Syria and Jordan who returned by their means.
“I decided to go to Syria after they killed several doctors in Baghdad,” said Sabah al-Qubaisi, a Sunni doctor.
“I came home today because I saw on TV and read in the newspapers what happened in Baghdad and since the city is safer now.”
“They say that the Mahdi army no longer commits crimes!” he said, referring to ecclesiastical activist Moqtada al-Sadr’s Shia militia, who forced his men to move away for now.
Qassim al-Atwani, a 38-year-old Shia teacher from the Sunni-dominated Amariyah area, came home with his two children, as he could no longer renew his Syrian visa and because the regime prevented Iraqis from working.
His neighbors from Shia to Amariyah called him to inform him that the militia helped by the United States made it possible to drive Al Qaeda out of the west of the capital, and invited him to return.
One of Jordan’s newcomers is the leader of a Sunni and Shia tribe who had to leave his home north of Baghdad because of a grenade attack after he incited citizens to rebel against Al Qaeda.
But he had to go back to fight.
“Two days ago, some of my friends called me and told me to have set up an anti-Al-Qaeda militia in Sleikh, and that they needed me and my boys.”
"That's why I'm coming back today," said Sabaah Alu Obeidi, 62.
Many say they’ve attracted by the capital’s growing lull since U.S. troops arrived, and the sight of continuous training of well-armed “citizen groups” and collaborating with the U.S. military to defend their neighborhood.
But still others say they have been forced to return because of the visa problem and the decadency of the capital of Damascus, where more than 1.3 million Iraqis have fled the past years.
Salaam al-Samarrai, 51, a father of three, expressed everyone's suspicion about exiled visas that Nouri al-Maliki, Prime Minister Shia, who allegedly asked the Syrian government to limit to force people to return home.
More than two and a half million people are said to have fled the country because of the sectarian squadrons of death.
“We didn’t have a problem until al-Maliki came to Syria and urged the government not to give visas to the Iraqis, to force them to return to Iraq,” said pensioner Sunni.
“I can’t trust a government that supports the criminals who burned down my house when I left.”
The drop in violence can be partly explained by the fact that the Sunnis have been driven out of areas of Shia and vice versa, creating places where tensions are less.
There are fears that a sudden flow of refugees will again increase sectarian tensions and bring back the fighting.
Little saar, good training.
The smallest federated state conducts vocational training, and even shines through the international orientation of its higher schools.
It is only in one area that a deficit weighs on the Saars, similar to that of the rest of the country.
"I explained yesterday for the 50th time to a non-Sarrois, that we are not French" - this is the slogan that the smallest federated state, by self-depreaning, displays for its 50th anniversary.
At this level, France and Luxembourg play an important role in the Saar training system, a system that is placed fifth in the training instructor of the German Institute of Economics in Cologne.
Thus, already by tradition, higher schools cooperate closely with neighbouring countries.
The university was founded in Saarbrücken in 1948 by the French.
To date, students have the opportunity to complete their studies there with a double or even a triple degree.
This is evidenced by the high amount of 15.6 percent foreign students.
The Saar also leads the children of the country to the upper schools in excess of the average number - many of them without a Gymnasium diploma.
"In the Saar, we take advantage of the vocational training system," says Axel Plünnecke, an expert in training at IW.
More than one in five young people obtain their title of studies in a vocational school, so the Saarland is more than seven points above the national average.
Also the possibilities of combining professional and academic training are relatively well developed.
In view of the number of inhabitants, the panel of double courses is the second best in Germany.
Despite hands-on training and growing demand, however, the number of engineering graduates has fallen by one-third in recent years.
This is why the Ministry of Education of Saarland has forced higher schools into a marketing campaign in the subjects of engineering sciences.
"After the first PISA studies, we celebrated a large number of reforms in the general schools," said a spokeswoman for the Ministry of Education.
Thus the government has fully introduced the federated state of comparison tests.
For children to start learning earlier, the last year of crÒrice is right-free - a pattern that meanwhile other federated states also copy.
Only future comparison studies will be able to show whether student performance improves sustainably.
In PISA studies in 2003, the amount of at-risk students decreased from 29 to 20 percent.
In total, however, the Saarland did not improve as much as other federated states.
The Bavarians are doing better.
And in spite of the Ságüörn, knowledge of foreign languages is not measured by PISA studies - one could otherwise earn points in this field.
Because the language of the neighbor is compulsory in high schools - even if sarrois are not French.
Chávez rules the watches
In Venezuela, watches will soon tick otherwise than in the rest of the world.
On December 9, President Hugo Chavez will raise the half-hour chronometer in the South American country.
Chavez has since become a caricature in Spain.
As the official newspaper of Venezuela communicated, because of a new law, the watches will be settled at less than four and a half hours from universal time.
In this way, the South American country obtains a time zone that does not exist in any other state in the world.
The anti-American Chávez justified his position, among other things, by the fact that the United States would have imposed on the world the separation of time zones in full hours.
In addition, Chávez announced Wednesday (local time) the cessation of relations with the neighboring country, Colombia, because of a case of hostages.
The left-wing nationalist announced that as long as conservative Alvaro Uribe remained president of Colombia, he would not maintain relations with either the head of state or the Colombian government.
"I can't, already only because of my dignity," said Chávez, who hastens a constitutional reform on which the citizens of the oil country will have to vote by referendum on Sunday.
With this reformation, it would significantly strengthen its power in the country.
The trigger of this dispute was Uribe's decision to withdraw the negotiating mandate in Chávez over the release of hostages with Marxist rebels in Colombia.
Uribe allegedly "spit in the face" of Venezuela with this decision, criticised Chávez on Sunday.
In Spain also Chávez is in every mouth.
A new mobile phone ringtone is fury in the country: "Por qué no te callas" (why don't you keep silent) is the phrase that already more than half a million people have uploaded to the internet.
It was pronounced by the King of Spain Juan Carlos who, at the most recent Ibero-American summit, was pissed by the constant arrests of left-wing populists and who told him to remain silent.
Wage cuts threaten Germany in 2007.
Despite the strong conjuncture, according to a prognosis, a real loss in salary threatens German employees this year.
Experts have cited all sorts of reasons to explain their pessimistic prognosis.
According to a calculation by the Hans-Böckler Foundation, employees are expected to expect in 2007 a real decrease on their pay slip compared to the previous year.
"The fault lies in the weak development of wages, the increase of the Value Added Tax and the rises in the prices of food and energy," Gustav Horn of the Institute for Macroeconomics and Conjuncture Research told the newspaper "Bild".
New expenses could also arise in terms of unemployment insurance for the employee.
Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück told "Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung" that the projected reduction in unemployment insurance of 3.3 percent for the new year was certainly correct and reasonable in the current situation.
However, the National Employment Agency no longer has the right to find itself embarrassed to ask the Federation for appropriations or financial aid, in the words of the SPD politician.
It was known Tuesday that the explosion in food and hydrocarbon prices had pushed the rate of inflation to its highest level in 13 years.
In totality, prices increased by 3.0 percent year-over-year.
Venus is the infernal binocular of the Earth.
Despite its atmosphere hostile to life, Venus looks more like the Earth than previously believed: the European "Venus Express" spacecraft has uncovered lighting in the atmosphere and whirlwinds at the poles, similar to those that appear in winter on Earth.
For almost three decades, scientists have assumed that there are lightning on Venus.
The European "Venus Express" spacecrone has now provided proof of this - it has detected electromagnetic waves at the edge of the atmosphere: lightning on Venus bursts at a height of about 56 kilometers of cloud cloud, reports Christopher Russel of the University of California in Los Angeles in the scientific magazine "Nature".
According to the famous cyprine theory, lightning would have made life appear on Earth: the thunderstorm stimulated reactions in the oceans that led to the formation of amino acids, the cornerstone of life.
That’s not the case on Venus: “If there’s been a lifetime, it must have been at a very old date in history,” says Sean Solomon of the “Carnegie Institution” in Washington.
There should be little living things today on the surface of Venus: the atmosphere is about a hundred times denser than on Earth, there are sometimes temperatures of 500 degrees.
"Venus may be the infernal twin of the Earth, but she's still a binocular," Russel said.
Why Venus, who so closely resemble the Earth en masse and dimensions, has been struck by such a different evolution, is the fundamental question of the ASE mission.
The departure of "Venus Express" in November 2005 was an important milestone in Venusian research, since no more probes were on its way to the exploration of this planet since the 1990s.
In the opinion of the researchers, the most recent results of research that have now been presented in several articles of "Nature" do not only underline the singularity of Venus within the solar system.
They also show that the commonalities with the Earth of our neighboring planet are more numerous than assumed at first glance.
Thus the researchers found, in addition to the wind vortices already known at the north pole, a whirlwind of clouds at the south pole, which rotates much faster.
The phenomenon is reminiscent of air currents in terrestrial hemispheres in winter, writes a team of researchers around Giuseppe Piccioni of the Istituto di Astrofisica Spaziale e Fisica Cosmica.
The second planet in the solar system has almost the same mass as the Earth and is, with a diameter of 12,100 kilometers, almost as large.
The planet described as an evening or morning star revolves around the sun in just 225 days.
However, it rotates much more slowly on itself: one Venusian day lasts 243 Earth days.
Although Venus, with surface temperatures of 460 degrees Celsius and a pressure of 92 bars, makes little reminiscent of the Earth, scientists assume that the two planets formed under similar conditions.
Out of both, the water accumulated and remained on Earth in the form of oceans, but was lost in space over Venus.
And while carbon dioxide has attached itself to Earth in the form of carbonate, it makes most of the atmosphere on Venus.
Köhler defends Merkel’s meeting with the Dalai Lama.
The President of the Federal Republic of Germany Köhler demanded an end to the party's quarrel over welcoming the Dalai Lama to the Chancellery.
Indirectly, he criticized the SPD because of the attacks on the chancellor after the meeting in September.
Federal President Horst Köhler cares about Germany's appearance and summons the grand coalition to close the quarrel over the Dalai Lama's reception.
"We must not present to the outside world a nation divided on this issue," Köhler told the Handelsblatt.
In parallel, Köhler showed understanding for Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) welcomed the Dalai Lama in September.
"Human rights have universal validity, even if I have respect for the differences between cultures," Köhler said.
Everyone knows that Germany should keep a significant balance on foreign policy, stressed the President of the Federal Republic, but defended Merkel against reproaches that she would organize a new policy of China.
The German chancellor did not distance herself from the traditional politics of a single China.
"China knows it too," Köhler said.
Foreign Minister Frank-Walter Steimeier (SPD) had indirectly expressed his dissatisfaction with the reception of the Dalai Lama and had been attacked for it outside the ranks of the CDU.
Steinmeier had complained in particular about the CDU's "stunting policy" on foreign policy.
Following the reception of the Dalai Lama at the Chancellery, virulent irritations were felt in the German-Chinese intercourse.
For example, the Chinese government has not only decommissioned German Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück (SPD), but according to information from the Spiegel, has also frozen the so-called strategic dialogue of the foreign ministers of the two countries.
Chinese Prime Minister Wen Jiabao is now demanding of German Chancellor Angela Merkel to acknowledge that welcoming the Dalai Lama was a mistake.
China refuses European politicians to receive the spiritual leader of Tibet in his official duties, supported Wen Jiabao as well as China's position.
Dalai Lama’s words and deeds would show that he is trying to separate Tibet from China and other Tibetan regions, which also encompasses other present-day provinces.
Schäuble warns against riots such as those in France.
Street fights with the police, shooting, burning cars: Wolfgang Schäuble warns against riots such as those of France.
Everything must be done so that social dissatisfaction in Germany does not break out in the streets through violence, demanded the German Minister of the Interior.
Barricades that burn, breakers shooting at the police with shotguns: after the severe riots of young people in France, the German Minister of the Interior Wolfgang Schäuble (CDU) fears a similar evolution in Germany.
We need to ensure that a social explosive does not constitute itself at home and defuses into violence.
We need to take this seriously," he told the "Neue Press" in Hanover.
Not all young individuals would be able to get away with the rapid transformations of social reality.
The Federation, the federated states and the municipalities were already working firmly to reduce the problems, said Schäuble.
It wouldn’t just affect the integration of immigrants.
"From integration summits to conferences on Islam, it already shows its early successes."
However, it would be too early for an end of alert.
Schäuble points out at the same time as in the German suburbs, there are no sociological problems or from the point of view of urban planning as in France.
In the northern Parisian agglomerations, young people had engaged in serious street battles with the police during the last nights.
In Toulouse too, cars and public buildings burned down.
Greater integration efforts on the part of the German government, this was demanded by the President of the Turkish communities in Germany, Kenan Kolat.
In view of the riots in France, Kolat warned against "imitation effects".
Certainly, the situation in Germany would not be as explosive as in France.
However, there would still be a need to act.
Kolat emphasises the fact that the quota for the unemployed among young people under the age of 25 with an immigrant background would be above average.
Above all, it would be necessary to ensure more places for training.
Kolat insisted: "We have to give young people perspective."
However, the president of the Turkish communities added that social control in the families took place more in Germany than in France.
Many young people from immigrant backgrounds also do not want to destroy what their parents have built here.
Kolat urged, “But we still have to be careful.”
Young people should "quickly be given the opportunity to participate in work and training".
In the meantime, in the Parisian suburb of Villiers-le-Bel, everything remained very quiet during the night.
Police units were in place, a helicopter flew over the area with spotlights.
Releasing authorities reported three arrests as well as a few cars and buns burned.
Since the conference, a deployment of about 1000 security forces ensures calm.
The police will intervene as long as necessary, announced the Minister of the Interior Michelle Alliot-Marie, after a visit to the suburb.
On the night of Tuesday, nearly 80 police officers were injured during riots in Villiers-le-Bel.
In the following night, the unrest overflowed in Toulouse.
Riots erupted after a traffic accident in which two youths lost their lives Sunday.
According to the first elements of the Public Prosecutor's Office, the police are not responsible for the death of young people.
In Angela Merkel's Empire.
The chancellor's utopian entry shows that the coalition is done with the background - and there's nothing to expect from her anymore.
In what country does this chancellor live exactly?
Following his distressing intervention in the general debates of the German Parliament, which was chaotic and poor of content, it must be said: not here - not in the country which should ultimately be governed by it.
The reproach of focusing too much on politics has been repelled by Angela Merkel with a harsh and presumptuous phrase.
It's provincial," she said, "has only showed that she doesn't want to understand what the critics want.
In Parliament, it has made visible to everyone how accurate this reproach is.
There was no concrete interest or feelings for the country's problems to be felt.
Instead, Angela presented herself as a cloud: a weightless politician, fleeing into foreign policy and even there stays up close.
For example, climate protection: in this regard, it welcomes the Heiligendam Agreements again and again, but does not say how the climate targets will have to be achieved in our country.
Even, the praise of oneself - having so little modesty, the leader of the CDU would have refused it again two years ago.
Now she's clinging to ready-made formulas that growth would affect people.
By this she destroys her image as a pragmatist, which solves problems instead of beautifying the world.
But in the Angela cloud empire, domestic policy problems are a marginal phenomenon.
Child poverty and the concerns of low-wage workers?
She still has a few ancillary sentences for that.
In this regard is indicative of the number of times she makes slips when it comes to family allowance.
Here, Merkel needs three starts to pronounce a half-precise phrase.
She staggers in uncharted territory, from the moment it becomes concrete.
The Attack Branch
In YouTube’s debate of Republican presidential candidates, the two favorites Rudy Giuliani and Mitt Romney engage in a tough offensive battle – and must now fear an outsider candidate.
The tension of the candidates is already erupting on the first subject.
He allegedly made New York a "refuge for the illegals", criticizes Mitt Romney for his opponent Rudy Giuliani.
"I'd say his own house was a haven for clandestine," Giuliani reproaches the former governor of Massachusetts for employing undocumented immigrants.
The candidates rise to the attack: since the Republican base also does not have official favorites, the eight presidential candidates use the debates on CNN above all to discredit their opponents.
The 30-second amateur movies that Internet users have sent via Youtube are barely more than blowers.
“It’s with this kind of isolationism that we let Hitler come to power,” McCain told his candidate Ron Paul, who is the only one in the round to call for an immediate withdrawal of Iraq.
Romney is also lectured vigorously by veterans of Vietnam, when he refuses to convict as torture what is called "Waterboarding", a method of interrogation where drowning is simulated.
However, the fact that the important themes of the campaign meanwhile go beyond the war in Iraq and debates on torture can be seen in the reactions of the common room in St. Petersburg, Florida.
Whether conservative values are leased or future tax hikes are denied, applause breaks out into the auditorium.
This is where the weaknesses of the two favorites live again.
"I made a mistake at that time," Romney contrites and apologises to the base and the televised public for still being a supporter of abortion in 1994 in 1994.
On the contrary, Giuliani passes into a snowing knot when asked his opinion on literal interpretations of the Bible.
Musharraf reigns for the first time in civilian clothes.
A day after his withdrawal as head of the armies, Pakistan's man of power Musharraf has been invaded as president.
Thus eight years of domination by the army ended.
Pakistan’s President Pervez Musharraf was invested for another five-year term on Thursday.
Pakistan’s television broadcast live the ceremony at the presidential palace of the capital Islamabad.
Musharraf vowed to respect the Constitution and to act for the well-being of the nation.
On Wednesday he transmitted his office as chief of the armies to his confidant Ashfaq Kiyani and had thus complied with international pressure, including that of the United States.
Eight years after his seizure of power by a bloodless putsch, the former general now reigns for the first time as chief of civil status.
With this withdrawal from the post of commander-in-chief of the armed forces, Musharraf had fulfilled the main requirement of the opposition and the West.
Another central requirement is the end of the state of emergency that Musharraf had decreed on November 3.
Already shortly after his inauguration Musharraf will suspend the state of emergency, according to Attorney General Malik Muhammad Qayyum, and thus will fulfill another requirement of the West.
The country's Supreme Court, which Musharraf had occupied after the establishment of a state of emergency with lawyers of its ranks, had rejected last week all appeals against the controversial re-election of the head of state on October 6.
Meanwhile, five soldiers were killed in the northern Tribal region of Northern Waziristan in an attack on a military convoy, and four others wounded.
According to the army's spokeswoman, a bomb exploded on a street near the border of Afghanistan.
Krautrock's fathers are coming back.
Germany's supergroups of the seventies replay: the three members of the band Harmonia again took their console in a concert in Berlin.
For their return have appeared recordings of their great success "Live 1974".
Already at that time, they were much more well-known abroad.
The expected sensation then occurs in the form of three gentlemen advertised at the evening poster.
Everyone has his little box in front of him, his cable and above all his buttons.
Dieter Moebius, 63, sits in the middle in a snow-slapping shirt.
He is flanked to the left by 57-year-old Michael Rother in possession of a laptop and guitar.
On the right, observed by the entire auditorium, Hans-Joachim Roedelius, 73, curves on his console.
Everyone contributes to the noise, among other things even a Beat.
And sometimes this is inadvertently added to a traditional piece of music.
That doesn't seem too much to bother the gentlemen themselves.
The fact that Harmonia plays again has ensured a few excitement among Krautrock enthusiasts.
We have the right to reveal it here: targeted polls reveal that, admittedly, the name reasons in the collective memory of pop, but in any case in such a sustainable way so that this band has been missing so painfully for 32 years.
But why wouldn't the German also have the right to celebrate his living legends of the seventies?
All the more because what is deceptibly called "Krautrock" has always rained more in England and America than in the country of origin.
When the music industry awarded in 2003 the Echo Prize to the survivors of the Cologne group Can for their work, we saw in the room the German pop faces interrogators.
When the Red Hot Chili Peppers also thanked the bands La Düsseldorf and Neu! in the panegyrical praise, the astonishment had no more limits.
Later, during concerts, Michael Rother jumped on the Red Hot Chili Peppers as an old friend.
The problem of ignorance at home and transfiguration into the unknown is the one and only.
Memory is therefore already taken by Harmonia, because the trio existed only two years and only during this period, the power of impact that was felt was much weaker than what was later attributed to it by others.
Moebius and Roedelius had already performed with artist Conrad Schnitzler under the very promising name of Cluster.
We first played in the cellars below the Berlin scene.
Then in Düsseldorf, for a label subsidized by the church, for financial reasons.
Rother had briefly troubleshooted Kraftwerk and had finally made a name for himself as a guitarist with Neu!, which was at the time perhaps the most radical German band.
Around 1973, however, Rother followed Cluster’s musicians into exile in Weserbergland forests.
There, in an ancient farmhouse in Weser, a 16th-century ruin, they lived as members of communities fleeing the city.
Less ideological than in the "Kommune I" community, where Roedelius had the right to be tormented as a nurse.
Rather an artistic existence like Spitzweg.
Supposedly, there was in the trash can a beacon of the choir Harmonia Ottenstein and also a name for art.
A lot kept Harmonia away from that.
The first album was called "Musik von Harmonia", and the following "De Luxe".
Because as serene and modest as the music sounded, the group suffered from material misery.
Harvesting dead wood alone was not used in search of itself.
The "Live 1974" CD that has just appeared was recorded during a concert in Griessem in front of a record audience of nearly 50 heads, as well as someone thinks they remember.
Rother issued at that time the desire to force Harmonia to train from time to time.
It was for Roedelius and Moebius an immoral requirement.
The group announced its separation in 1975.
Rother landed in the spheres of esotericism and assured his existence with albums such as "Flammende Herzen" (flaming hearts).
Cluster revived in the eighties and has been more successful than Harmonia has ever had.
Griessem's Live album to Herbert Grönemeyer.
His Grönland label has set himself the goal of maintaining Krautrock as a German contribution inherent in contemporary music.
Neu! had already been dug up on this occasion as the personal work of Roedelius.
The physical resurrection of Harmonia takes place at the House of World Cultures in Berlin.
Subsidized publicly as part of the Woldtronics Festival.
Julian Cope is here, the British, former punk and author of the paperback book "Krautrock Sampler".
There's also the inventor of Loveparade, Dr. Motte.
By which the entire fluctuating margin of Krautrock's influence would be identified with regard to future pop music.
The place left empty by the group will be filled by Harmonia.
Brian Eno’s sentence hovers in the room in the middle of singular sounds: “The only real music, you listen to the future.”
There are pictures that show producers with Harmonia in the farm room.
Screenings of other black-and-white photos of bazaar-wired tools are used for the formation of the caption.
In front, the three veterans are once again busy improvising.
But more by sticking microphones to the organ and the drums, to pull to the instruments unheard of sounds thanks to the instruments with effects built yourself.
Electronic music looks like electronic music today.
The three in great technical fignors.
Even the choir of the house will not change anything.
Under the direction of Barbara Morgenstern, we cough and whisper under the cloud of sounds, and we sing at a nice moment "Aaaaaaah" with emphasis.
Krautrock has always been a very English concept.
It's never been rock.
Roots have been less searched in the blues than in music conservatories (Can), technologies (Kraftwerk) or in the flight of artistic or social (Cluster) conventions.
He who shouts "68" is not totally wrong, and Harmonia also no longer allows himself to be irritated by this now.
But what goes most of itself will be covered meticulously on the scene.
Where does the music start, and where does it end?
It can hit the nerves of someone (?) or give fleeting moments of great joy.
Richard Gere isn't cynical enough.
With "The Hunting Party" and "Lethal Peace", two films once again address the Balkan War.
But the two don’t convince.
Either the projects rely too much on the action and on the exchanges of senseless shots or, on the contrary, they are too watery.
Here, even Richard Gere in "The Hunting Party" can't help.
The good news first: certainly war criminals Karadzic and Mladic are still hiding in the mountains of the Balkans, but it is precisely there, in the most gloomy hinterland of the Bosnian war, that actor Richard Gere is then found.
We had last lost sight of the finest man of the eighties, because far too many nice and futile roles in women's sentiment films and far too many press conferences on the theme of Buddhism delighted him with the desire to show themselves.
Today Richard Gere is suddenly the most important argument to still watch "The Hunting Party" - when the hunter becomes hunted (why must today's films be as long and confused as collective agreements of locomotive drivers?)
He delivers a magnificent representation full of winks: at bottom, his war reporter character Simon Hunt is what Gere could have become, if fate and the film industry were not as benevolent: a man who has had his best moments for a long time behind him, but who, even as a ruined monument to his decline, nevertheless kept visible remains of his former charm.
But despite this asset, "The Hunting Party" continues together with the German production "Peace deadly", which is also released this week, the endless sequence of failed films about the Balkan war.
"Deadly peace" one can quickly make the trial: the film speaks of two German soldiers in Kosovo (Adrian Topol, Max Riemelt), who saves a Serbian from the Kosovar snipers, in which one of the two still quickly falls in love.
Although it is welcome that here the Serbs are for once not presented as the only causes of all the ailments, this film as flat as a television film is lost far too quickly in the action and the exchange of senseless shots.
This remains a kind of "Alarme for Cobra 11" without a highway but with a war instead.
On the contrary, we can examine in the perfect example of "The Hunting Party", why deep down there has never been a real good film about the Balkan war.
The story of the three U.S. war reporters, who condition themselves to track the high war criminal "the fox", could perhaps have become, along with another director and another screenwriter (both Richard Shepard), at another moment a new "Apocalypse Now".
Because the stories are strikingly similar: two males go as a small team in the forest, to accomplish something that the military can’t do and for the two males, it’s becoming a journey into insanity.
One of the reasons for the failure of "The Hunting Party" is that we simply don't let Simon Hunt be the cynical deserted endowed with a heart, as it's presented to us at the beginning.
No, the script must also attribute to him a romantic adventure with a Bosniak murdered afterwards.
Then "The Hunting Party" becomes definitively evil, when the reporter, in the space of the last five minutes, by an accelerated process, manages that NATO, the CIA and all bounty hunters of the world have not succeeded in five years: catching the war criminal and leaving him for a fair revenge to the Bosnian Muslims.
It's so bad that we should go to the Hague International Court of Crime against Cinematographic Logic.
In this case, the film has too deep human concern.
He wants to denounce that Karadzic and Mladic are still at large, and he suspects the Americans and the French (here they must exceptionally agree) not to drive hunting quite intensively due to secret political agreements.
But the well-meaning and the villain are so similar this time that a famous poem by Brecht comes to our mind.
The one speaks of a wooden mask of an Asian demon on the working table of the poet: "Compassionate, I see the veins of the forehead swollen, clearly: as it's tiring to be wicked."
Movies like “The Hunting Party” have exactly such tumescent heads, but that insinuate how tiring it’s to be.
The problem is not limited to the kind of films about the Balkan War.
Recently, it has been reported in this newspaper that all Hollywood films about Iraq were big flops.
We can add: there have never been a good movies about South Africa before.
This is true every time: when a star hangs out on an outbreak of crisis or on a minefield, there is then something like "Rangoon" with Patricia Arquette, "Jenseits going Grenzen" with Angelina Jolie or ""Blutdiamanten" with Leonardo DiCaprio.
It’s possible that all these conflicts are still simply too close in time.
Anyway, decades have always passed before Hollywood could make a good movie about the holocaust with "Schindler's List."
Some films still have to mature - those that are now worth classic works on Vietnam "The Deer Hunter", "Apocalypse Now" or "Full Metal Jackett" have in no way been unanimously celebrated in their time as masterpieces.
But perhaps we know today also simply too much about the conflicts of the world, where every hecatomb arrives live on two screens at the same time at home,
And this thrills screenwriters the lack of scruples they need to tell beautiful stories.
"Being or not being" or "The Dictator" have actually become so good also because Lubtisch and Chaplin did not yet know how the Nazi regime really dominated.
Maybe you shouldn't want to be good when you want to make good movies.
Perhaps we must have the sarcastic and distanced artistic look of the novelist Lawrence Norfolk.
When asked about the parallel between his historical novel "Dictionary of Lemprière" and the Balkan War, he replied: "It's only due to the fact that certain things never change."
"Big cities will always be horribly dangerous, men will always be murdered in a horrifying way and war will always reign in Yugoslavia."
Von Beust collects criticism for the cancellation of the Olympiades.
The Mayor's recoil with Hamburg's candidacy for the Summer Olympics clashed in politics and the economy with virulent critics.
Many want to keep fighting for the Waterfront Games.
But Von Beust calls for realism in the appreciation of opportunities.
Hans-Jörg Schmidt-Trenz, senior director of the Hamburg Chamber of Commerce, on Wednesday described an abandonment as "bad for the local economy".
Lately, no other project had deployed as many motivating forces in the city as the idea of the Olympiads.
"Without this objective, political-sporty momentum could not have been maintained in the same measures."
Schmidt-Trenz urges the Senate not to bury the project, but to find it a new longer-term starting point, "which takes as a starting point another modified agenda."
Mayor Ole von Beust remained in his previous statements on Wednesday that Hamburg's Olympic ambitions were unrealistic at least until 2028.
"It's not Hamburg who decides on a candidacy for the Olympiads, but the German Olympic Sports Federation," Von Beust told WELT ONLINE.
It refers to the expected decision of the Sports Federation at the beginning of December, about Munich's candidacy for the 2018 winter games.
This preference means for us that, in the amount of time that we had planned until 2028 so far, we won’t get a chance.”
However the dream doesn’t definitely belong to the past, Von Beust said.
The Hamburg Sports Federation (HSB) regretted Beust’s statements.
From this the impression would be given that Hamburg no longer wants to apply for the Olympic Games in the next 25 years.
“It’s too early to close an opportunity, burying the plans already now,” HSB President Günter Ploß said on Wednesday.
An abandonment of the candidacy would also be in contradiction with the statements of the head of the Senate on the occasion of the reception in plenary assembly of the German Federation of Higher Schools of Sport.
There, Von Beust reportedly described Hamburg's bid for the 2015 Universiades as "not a substitute" for the Olympics in Hamburg.
The Federation of Higher Schools of Sport had first in the past weeks named Hamburg as a candidate city.
The mayor’s SPD faction blamed the Senate for not using this valuable time in housing construction in Hamburg.
"The development of the Petit Grasbrook was rejected by the Senate for years because it played the Olympiad card and excluded the construction of housing," said SPD delegates Carola Veit and Jan Quast.
While the SPD has always demanded for the Petit Grasbrook an integrated plan in the Olympiades and a subsequent use of dwellings, the CDU would have relied exclusively on the "Event factor".
The bid for the 2012 Summer Olympics would, for its part, have shown great enthusiasm in Hamburg.
Hamburg, however, had broken up against Leipzig.
They'ren't Warhols.
The one that connects Sweden concepts and wooden crate, thinks of Ikea, but not Andy Warhol.
Or if?
At the moment, wooden crates made in Sweden cast shadows on Warhol’s list of works.
A large part of the ninety Brillo-Boxes of "Type Stockholm" listed there are forgeries.
After the Swedish newspaper "Espressen" questioned a few months ago the authenticity of Andy Warhol's 1968 Brillo-Boxes, the internal searches of the Moderna Museet in Stockholm lead today to a disenchaming result: the six "Brillo-Boxes", of which the former director of the Pontus Hulten museum made a gift in 1995 at the Moderna Museet, are copies - as well as
Ancient history is entangled, a disconcerting sequence of artistic and curatoric productions.
In 1964, Warhol created his first "Brillo-Boxes", painted wooden crates, which are based on the red-blue-white design of "Brillo" powdered soap cartons.
An exhibition of his work at the Moderna Museet presented in 1968 - with Warhol's agreement - hundreds of Brillo boxes made of cardboard: these did not come (for financial reasons) from Warhol, but had been directly delivered from the Brillo cardboard factory.
These were not works of art, but rather their models, exhibition samples.
At the same time, Warhol had given the authorization in 1968 to produce a few Brillo wooden crates in Sweden, which were moreover not exhibited at the Moderna Museet.
Witnesses of the time today talk about about fifteen wooden crates which at the time would have entered into the history of art and auctions, as original Brillo-Boxes of Andy Warhol authorized, with the appellation "Stockhock type".
Some of them have reached more than US$100 000 in auctions.
The problem now is that the legendary director of the Pontus Hultén Museum falsely claimed later that about one hundred wooden crates had been produced and exhibited in Sweden in 1968 that Warhol would then have ceded to him.
In truth, the 105 crates of Hultén were produced in Malmö only in 1990, for an exhibition in St. Petersburg.
Warhol had been dead for three years.
Lars Byström, chief curator of the Moderna Museet, examined, alongside the museum's six Brillo-Boxes, three others from Swedish private collections, two of which were made in Stockholm in 1968: their laminated panels were first printed and sanded, then applied with a white oil paint brush.
On the contrary, the texture of the copies dating back to 1990 reveals that the painting has been directly applied without a layer of background on the laminated panels, with the help of a roll of paint - in addition it is not an oil painting, but an acrylic.
“These crates have not been authorized by the artist and should be removed from Andy Warhol’s official Brillo-Boxes list,” says Moderna Museet director Lars Nittve: “If the ‘Andy Warhol Art Authentication Board’ in New York doesn’t come from another decision, the Moderna Museet will classify the six crates in its collection as copies.”
Already in July the Authentication Board has of course announced, that it will investigate the "heavy accusations" that will be raised in the case of soap powder cases.
But since then we've had an inexorable silence there.
Manila Rebellious Soldiers Abandon
In the Philippines, the rebel soldiers are abandoning, the same who had entrenched themselves in a hotel in Manila, who had demanded the withdrawal of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
Chief Antonio Trillanes, who accused the President of corruption and electoral fraud, told reporters in the hotel that he could not answer for any bloodshed.
Soldiers loyal to power had shortly before stormed the hotel using tear gas.
The two dozen soldiers had been entrenched in the building for hours.
Approximately half of them were standing in court on Wednesday because of a failed coup attempt in 2003 – among them Trillanes.
He took advantage of the audience to suddenly eclipse with about 30 supporters.
The military police officers, who normally had to monitor them, had apparently deserted in his favor and had joined his mutiny.
They broke into the Peninsula Hotel and demanded the removal of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
Loyal troops shut down the area around the respectable hotel.
The President convened an urgent meeting of the Security Cabinet.
National Security Adviser Norberto Gonzales condemned the action of the soldiers.
"People are tired of it," Trillanes said.
"We're calling on people to join us."
General Danilo Lim, who commanded prior to the Scouts Rangers elite unit, said that other units in the army soon wanted to join the march.
Critics of the regime had called for a major protest against the President on Wednesday.
His government has been surrounded for months by reproaches of corruption.
Manila's financial district had already been since 2003 the scene of the mutiny attempt of about 200 members of the army against Arroyo.
The insurgency broke nearly 24 hours after the army refused to join.
Since 1986 there have already been at least seven coup attempts against democratically elected governments in the Philippines.
United Internet becomes a major shareholder in Versatel.
United Internet has secured more than 20 percent stake in the telecommunications company Versatel.
The average price per share is 14.46 euros, as communicated by the company on Wednesday.
Only 20.05 per cent of the share capital would be held.
We had previously speculated about a buyout.
The option would remain open to further increase participation in Versatel.
With this step we seek to accelerate the consolidation of the German DSL market, said United Internet.
Just before, assumptions had already been made Wednesday about an entry, after Berenberg bank increased its stake in the Düsseldorf network operator from 5 percent to 15.1 percent.
The credit institute would hold the money only for a third part, it says on the market.
The day before, United Internet had already acquired a stake of nearly 10 percent in the mobile phone company Drillisch.
As part of a capital increase, Drillisch placed 17.4 million new equities worth 106.4 million euros.
United Internet has bought 5.15 million for 31.1 million euros.
In addition, the companies opened negotiations on cooperation in the commercialization of DSL and mobile telephony products.
“We’re strengthening our partnership,” a United Internet spokesperson said.
Both companies founded the MSP equity firm in order to absorb competitor Freenet.
For the moment, the two companies want to consider themselves satisfied with their ten percent participation at Freenet, and not make use of their right to endorse 18.49 per cent more at Freenet, United Internet said Thursday.
The two companies, however, would allow themselves all open options, especially to increase participation in the SA Freenet, it was learned.
Negotiations between United Internet and Freenet over a strategic partnership ended in mid-October.
Poking that is still difficult to predict on Freenet, which has been going on for months, is part of the consolidation of the German telecoms market.
At the end of the third quarter, Freenet had 2.9 million customers bound by a mobile phone contract and 1.27 million DSL customers and complete subscriptions.
The Holding of United Internet and Drillisch previously held a 6.01 percent direct stake in Freenet.
It has now been agreed that Drillisch would bring about an additional four percent.
The right to buy 18.49 percent of the investment company Vatas had already been decided in August.
